Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Rootstocks Infiltrate the Copper Relay
- Story & Context: During the brutal winter storm of 1998 at Vardø Lighthouse, keeper Torstein Lind wedged his grandmother’s battered banjo between two sparking transceiver terminals to ground an electrical fault, inadvertently transmitting high-gain clawhammer harmonics across the Barents maritime distress channel. The smell of scorched sheep’s wool insulation filled the lantern room as nearby crab trawler crews suddenly heard frantic acoustic picking slammed beneath 165-BPM diesel-pump kick thuds blasting through their navigation monitors. Torstein refused to cut the circuit even as generator sparks singed his beard, roaring into the foghorn horn: "The root breaks the beacon before the sea takes the hull!" That illicit recording circulated for years on magnetic tape among coastal deckhands before becoming the founding scripture of the scene.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro close-up album art showing thick, gnarled plant roots breaking through a rusted copper circuit board inside a lighthouse beacon, glowing vacuum tubes illuminating dirt and tender green sprouts, dark industrial machinery entwined with wild brambles, cinematic lighting, gritty 35mm grain.

Track 2: Whose Hand Cut the Scion?
- Story & Context: Holed up in the damp greenhouse lean-to bolted against the south face of the Sula Skerry tower, botanist-turned-radio-operator Astrid Holm cultivated frost-resistant root vegetables under buzzing sodium-arc lights while nursing a bitter feud with the mainland supply officer. To drown out the wind shrieking through the cracked glass panes, she patched her autoharp into an overdriven industrial compressor, hammering the low strings until the concrete foundations shuddered with concussive sub-bass. "I severed the graft so the stem could finally scream," Astrid confessed into the open beacon microphone, weeping openly between explosive salvos of 168-BPM distorted kicks that completely drowned out incoming harbor traffic. A stranded cargo ship recorded the twenty-minute audio assault, transforming her breakdown into the genre's most notorious dancefloor confession.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Interior shot of an old foggy greenhouse at night glowing under an intense amber sodium lamp, cracked glass panes covered in frost, an antique autoharp lying across damp potting soil with thick black patch cables running into heavy industrial speakers, surreal and melancholy atmosphere.

Track 3: Gunnar’s Sunless Hotbed
- Story & Context: Long after the complete automation of the Nordkapp beacon, local cod fishermen continued to report phantom gabber kicks thumping beneath the frozen waves whenever the aurora borealis lit the rusted iron cupola. Myth holds that the station's final keeper, an eccentric herbalist named Gunnar, layered heirloom seed potatoes and broken dulcimer strings inside the foghorn's pneumatic chamber before vanishing on foot across the pack ice. Modern coastal divers inspecting the underwater foundations still claim the seawater vibrates with a physical, rib-shattering 162-BPM thud that leaves the faint taste of pine resin and battery acid in their mouthpieces. Scrawled inside the foghorn compressor room, Gunnar's parting chalk inscription remains the movement's defining proverb: "Nothing ripens in the dark until the floorboards shake."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An abandoned concrete sea fortress jutting out of an icy Arctic ocean under a surreal violet and green aurora, ghostly green light radiating from underground ventilation grates, wild tundra ferns bursting unnaturally through frozen cracks in the concrete, mythic and foreboding.

